<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakbe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakbe is a Maya archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirador-basin">Mirador Basin</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, about 13 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mirador">El Mirador</a>. One of the earliest Maya cities, it had large platforms and temples in the Middle Preclassic period, from about 800 BCE, among the oldest monumental buildings in the Maya lowlands, and it was linked to El Mirador by a causeway. It declined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakbe.jpg" alt="Nakbe" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakbe,_small_temple_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nakbe</b> had large platforms and temples by about 800 BCE, and El Mirador, at its height from the 3rd century BCE, covered some 16 square kilometres; its La Danta complex rises about 72 metres above the forest floor, and raised causeways linked the city to Nakbe and its…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Stucco masks of deities decorated platform façades, and raised causeways (sakbeob) linked El Mirador with <b>Nakbe</b>, Tintal and other sites, suggesting a regional state.</blockquote><a href="/state/el-mirador">El Mirador</a></li></ul></section>
